  fill-in 'fill1' (l = 5) is consistent on 1.56% of paths; on those, min over paths of [l - (L_n(nu_H) - delta*)] = 0.936 (>= 0)

(L) leak: u_i = y_i with probability 0.8, seen by the predictor 'leak' -- (Priv) fails; n = 4 exact
  total path probability = 1.000000000000000;  M_0 = 0.90625
  E[M_n], n = 1..4: 0.9288, 0.9593, 1.0010, 1.0576   (grows: not a supermartingale)
  P[max_k M_k >=  2.0] = 0.13205   M_0/a = 0.45312   ok
  P[max_k M_k >=  4.0] = 0.03640   M_0/a = 0.22656   ok
  P[max_k M_k >=  8.0] = 0.00522   M_0/a = 0.11328   ok
  P[max_k M_k >= 16.0] = 0.00219   M_0/a = 0.05664   ok

(L') leak, simulated: class {human, leak}, 2000 runs of n = 1000 (seed 101)
  P_emp[delta* >=  1] = 1.0000   M_0 2^-j = 1.56e-01
  P_emp[delta* >=  4] = 0.9995   M_0 2^-j = 1.95e-02
  P_emp[delta* >= 16] = 0.9975   M_0 2^-j = 4.77e-06
  P_emp[delta* >= 32] = 0.9845   M_0 2^-j = 7.28e-11
  P_emp[delta* >= 64] = 0.8750   M_0 2^-j = 1.69e-20
  median delta* = 100.3 bits (expected gain of 'leak' about n*0.098 - 4 = 94)

(X) XOR world: y = x XOR z, calibrated human nu_H(1|x) = 1/2; z independent of y, x independent of y
  n =    10: the (x,z)-product predictor's gain minus its length =     5.86 bits, deterministically; P[delta* >= 5] = 1 > 2^-5
  n =    50: the (x,z)-product predictor's gain minus its length =    45.28 bits, deterministically; P[delta* >= 45] = 1 > 2^-45
  n =   100: the (x,z)-product predictor's gain minus its length =    94.55 bits, deterministically; P[delta* >= 94] = 1 > 2^-94
  n =  1000: the (x,z)-product predictor's gain minus its length =   981.50 bits, deterministically; P[delta* >= 981] = 1 > 2^-981
  so (Priv) must be conditional on the human's input (and on all side information), as Lemma 2.1 states; marginal independence of the coin from each variable is not enough.
